1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 Welcome to MemodoAI.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,510
 In the next 20 minutes or so, we'll walk through everything you need to be

3
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:12,000
 productive on your first day.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,660
 The basics, the few features that surprise people, and the workflows that actually

5
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:19,000
 pay off once you've used it for a week.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
 MemodoAI is Memodo's internal AI workspace.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 If you've used ChatGPT or Claude before, this will feel familiar.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,300
 The main difference: it runs on infrastructure we control, so internal data stays

9
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:34,000
 inside Memodo's environment.

10
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 You can use it for the obvious things: drafting emails, summarizing documents,

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
 explaining unfamiliar concepts, polishing rough notes.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,860
 And for a few less obvious things: chatting with your own files, building small

13
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:52,000
 agents tailored to a recurring task,

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 and rendering interactive artifacts inline in the chat.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 Two ground rules before we dive in:

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 MemodoAI is great at being a fast first drafter.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 It is not a source of truth.

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
 Always check facts that matter.

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,380
 And, when you're working with sensitive data, the principle of "use the minimum you

20
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,000
 need" still applies.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 We'll come back to this when we talk about PII warnings later.

22
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 The interface has three regions, left to right.

23
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
 On the far left is a column of icons.

24
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 Your control panel.

25
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 From top to bottom.

26
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 Open or close the sidebar.

27
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 Start a new chat.

28
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 Your chat history.

29
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 The agent builder.

30
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 Prompts.

31
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 Memories.

32
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 Bookmarks.

33
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:39,000
 Attach files.

34
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 Parameters.

35
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 And at the very bottom, your account avatar.

36
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 Hover over any of them to see its name.

37
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,170
 To the right of those icons is the conversation sidebar, where your past chats are

38
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:53,000
 grouped by recency.

39
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 And the big middle column is where you add your chat.

40
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 The column is where you actually work.

41
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 The top bar shows the active model or agent.

42
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 The composer at the bottom is where you type.

43
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
 The composer has four buttons that shift its behavior.

44
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 A paperclip for attaching files.

45
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 A sliders icon for tweaking parameters.

46
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
 A search button for the web.

47
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 And a file search button for chatting with documents.

48
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
 We'll touch each of these in turn.

49
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 Let's send our first message.

50
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,670
 The default model is GPT-5 and unless you have a reason to change it, that's the

51
00:02:27,670 --> 00:02:30,000
 right choice for almost everything.

52
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 I'll type a question.

53
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 Something you might actually ask on a workday.

54
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
 What's the difference between OKRs and KPIs?

55
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
 Give me a clear explanation I can share with my team.

56
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 I'll send that.

57
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 Notice the conversation auto-titles itself once the model finishes.

58
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 It picks something descriptive based on the content.

59
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
 You can always rename it manually by hovering over the title in the sidebar.

60
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 Now, under each assistant response, you'll see a row of icons.

61
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 From left to right.

62
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 Read aloud.

63
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,000
 Copy.

64
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 Edit.

65
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 Fork.

66
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
 Which means continue from this point in a new branch of the conversation.

67
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 Thumbs up, thumbs down, and regenerate.

68
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
 One quick tip while we're here.

69
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
 If you don't like a response, it's almost always better to edit your original

70
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 message and resend

71
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 than to argue with the model in a follow-up.

72
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,430
 Click the pencil icon on your own message, refine the question, and you'll get a

73
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:30,000
 cleaner answer in fewer turns.

74
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
 Same conversation, less noise.

75
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 Click the model name in the top bar to open the picker.

76
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,000
 You'll see two groups.

77
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 The first is MemodoAI.

78
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 That's the base models available in your instance.

79
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 Right now, that's GPT-5 with document upload support.

80
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
 The second is My Agents.

81
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
 These are pre-configured assistants that you or a colleague have built.

82
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,770
 Picking one of these from the model picker is the fastest way to start a chat with

83
00:03:56,770 --> 00:03:58,000
 that agent.

84
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 The rule of thumb for which to pick.

85
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:07,000
 Leave it on GPT-5 unless you're doing something an agent was specifically built for.

86
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
 There are two things in MemodoAI that look similar but solve different problems.

87
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 Presets and Agents.

88
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 Presets are saved bundles of model parameters.

89
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,000
 Things like:

90
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 Temperature

91
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 Max Tokens

92
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 Frequency Penalty

93
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 They live behind the Presets button, next to the model picker.

94
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 The defaults that MemodoAI ships with have been chosen for sensible behavior.

95
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
 Unless you have a specific reason to tweak them, leave them alone.

96
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,300
 Changing temperature without a clear hypothesis usually makes the model worse, not

97
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:40,000
 better.

98
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 Agents, on the other hand, are full personas.

99
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
 They have a name, an icon, a system prompt

100
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
 that defines how they behave, and optional tools.

101
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 Use an agent when the same role keeps coming up.

102
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
 We'll build one in a few minutes.

103
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 The short version.

104
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
 Presets are an advanced power user feature.

105
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 Ignore them if you're new.

106
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
 Agents are what you'll actually use day to day.

107
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 That dotted icon in the top right corner toggles temporary chat mode.

108
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:16,000
 When it's on,

109
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 the composer gets a purple ring and the conversation is not saved to your history.

110
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 Use this for one-off questions you don't want sitting in your sidebar.

111
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 Quick definitions.

112
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
 Throwaway brainstorms.

113
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
 Sensitive drafts.

114
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
 Closing the tab also ends it.

115
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,000
 Anything you send in temporary chat is gone the moment the chat closes.

116
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
 The model is trained on data that's months old by the time you're using it.

117
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
 To pull in fresh information,

118
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 toggle the search button on.

119
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
 It's the globe icon in the composer.

120
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
 I'll ask a question that needs current data.

121
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 The model decomposes the question into multiple web searches automatically.

122
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
 You'll see a Used N tools indicator at the top of the response.

123
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 Click it to see exactly what the model searched for and what it found.

124
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 Search is great for anything time sensitive.

125
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:11,000
 Pricing.

126
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 News.

127
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,000
 Current versions.

128
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
 Recent events.

129
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,300
 It struggles with very specific local data, niche corporate information, and

130
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:21,000
 anything behind a paywall.

131
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
 If a search comes back unclear, the model will usually offer to refine, guide it,

132
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,000
 name a source, narrow the scope, or specify what kind of answer you want.

133
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 This is one of the most useful features in MemodoAI.

134
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,050
 You attach a document, ask questions about it, and the model answers with citations

135
00:06:42,050 --> 00:06:43,000
 back to the source.

136
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
 Step 1. Turn on file search.

137
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 Click the toggle in the composer.

138
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
 Without this, the paperclip only lets you upload images.

139
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 Step 2. Attach the document.

140
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
 Click the paperclip.

141
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
 Now you have two options.

142
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
 Pick upload document.

143
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
 MemodoAI supports the formats you'd expect from a modern office workflow.

144
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,000
 PDFs.

145
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
 Word documents.

146
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:10,000
 Markdown.

147
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,000
 Plain text.

148
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 CSVs.

149
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:13,000
 Spreadsheets.

150
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
 Slide decks.

151
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
 And most common source code formats.

152
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
 There's a size limit around 25 megabytes per file.

153
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
 If a file gets rejected, split it first.

154
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
 Step 3. Ask.

155
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
 I'm going to ask.

156
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,000
 What are the three Q3 priorities and who owns each one?

157
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:32,000
 Cite the document.

158
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
 Notice the response.

159
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
 Each claim is tied back to the source.

160
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
 That's the citation behavior.

161
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 And the Searched Your Files expander shows the actual chunk of the file.

162
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
 It shows the actual chunks of the document the model read.

163
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
 You can verify any claim by clicking through.

164
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,000
 Two things to know.

165
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
 1. You can attach multiple documents to one chat.

166
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
 The model will search across all of them.

167
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,000
 2. For long documents, ask focused questions.

168
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:07,000
 Who owns priority 2 is much better than "Tell me about this doc."

169
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
 The narrower your question, the better the citation behavior.

170
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,000
 Agents are pre-configured assistants, someone built for a specific job.

171
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,640
 They have a name, an icon, a system prompt, and, optionally, some tools wired up

172
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:23,000
 like file search or web search.

173
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
 Let's start by using a shared agent.

174
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
 From the model picker, I'll pick Memodo Engineering Concierge.

175
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:36,210
 The chat area changes to show the agent's landing page, its name, its icon, and a

176
00:08:36,210 --> 00:08:38,000
 short description of what it's for.

177
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,000
 When I send a message,

178
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
 the agent uses its tools to find an answer.

179
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:47,000
 Notice the "Used N Tools" line at the top of the response.

180
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
 Same idea as web search.

181
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
 Just with the agent's own tools.

182
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:56,000
 Agents that have been built by other people, like this one, are read-only.

183
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
 You can use them, but not edit their instructions.

184
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
 Now, let's build our own.

185
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
 I'll open the agent builder.

186
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 That's the robot icon in the left rail.

187
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
 If I had a read-only agent selected,

188
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:13,000
 I'd see a "You don't have access to edit this agent" message.

189
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,000
 I'll click "Create new agent" at the top.

190
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,000
 The blank form has fields for the name, a short description, the category, the

191
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:21,000
 instructions,

192
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 which is the system prompt, the model, and the capabilities.

193
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
 The instructions are the heart of an agent.

194
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
 Be specific.

195
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,000
 What input it expects.

196
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
 What output it should produce.

197
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
 What tone to use and what it must not do.

198
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:42,000
 For our example, I'll create a meeting notes polisher.

199
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
 It takes rough bullet point notes and produces clean, structured minutes.

200
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
 The system prompt tells it exactly what sections to include:

201
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
 attendees, key decisions, action items, discussion summary, open questions.

202
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
 It tells it to mark missing fields as TBD.

203
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 And critically, it forbids inventing facts that aren't in the source.

204
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
 I'll pick GPT-5 as the model.

205
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:10,000
 Azure OpenAI as the provider.

206
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
 Click "Create".

207
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
 Now I can use it.

208
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:18,000
 I'll start a new chat with the meeting notes polisher and paste in some rough notes.

209
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
 The agent follows its instructions exactly.

210
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
 Structured sections.

211
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:24,000
 Owners.

212
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,000
 And deadlines.

213
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,000
 Extracted.

214
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,000
 TBD where the source didn't specify.

215
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
 No invented facts.

216
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 Three rules for writing good agent instructions, by the way.

217
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,000
 One.

218
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
 Describe input and output explicitly.

219
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
 The model follows shape more reliably than vibes.

220
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,000
 Two.

221
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
 Forbid the failure modes.

222
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 Never invent facts NOT in the source.

223
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
 Is more important than describing what right looks like.

224
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
 Three.

225
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
 Set the tone in one sentence.

226
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
 The model will hold the tone if you tell it once.

227
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
 There's one more agent capability worth showing.

228
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
 Artifacts.

229
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
 When you ask MemodoAI for something visual,

230
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 a chart,

231
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
 a small UI mockup,

232
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,000
 a styled table,

233
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
 an interactive widget,

234
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
 the result can be rendered as an artifact.

235
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,550
 A self-contained interactive panel that appears next to the chat instead of as a

236
00:11:17,550 --> 00:11:19,000
 plain code block.

237
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 To use it, the agent needs artifacts enabled.

238
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
 In the Agent Builder, scroll the Capabilities section.

239
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 You'll see two toggles:

240
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,000
 Enable Artifacts and Include shadcn/ui components instructions.

241
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,000
 The second toggle tells the agent about a popular component library,

242
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 so its artifacts look cleaner.

243
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
 Once enabled, ask for something visual.

244
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:47,000
 I'm going to ask the Meeting Notes Polisher to render the action items as an

245
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
 interactive HTML checklist,

246
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
 with checkboxes, owner labels, and a progress counter at the top.

247
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,000
 There it is.

248
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
 A side panel artifact.

249
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,000
 Fully interactive.

250
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
 I can check items off,

251
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
 the progress counter updates,

252
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
 and I can switch between

253
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:06,000
 the rendered preview and the underlying code with the toggle at the top.

254
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
 Artifacts are best for one-off prototypes and visual answers.

255
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:13,000
 The state inside an artifact isn't saved.

256
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
 If you check items off, that's just for the current view.

257
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
 MemodoAI remembers context across chats,

258
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
 so you don't have to keep reintroducing yourself.

259
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,000
 Things like your name,

260
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,000
 your role,

261
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
 ongoing projects you mentioned,

262
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
 recurring people you work with.

263
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
 These get quietly saved.

264
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
 Click the brain icon in the left rail to see what's saved.

265
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
 Each memory has a key,

266
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:37,000
 a short identifier,

267
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:38,000
 a value,

268
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
 the actual content,

269
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:41,000
 a token count,

270
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
 and a date.

271
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
 You can edit any of them by clicking the pencil icon,

272
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
 or delete with the trash icon.

273
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:52,370
 The Use Memory toggle at the top of the panel controls whether the model has access

274
00:12:52,370 --> 00:12:54,000
 to memories during your chats.

275
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
 Turn it off if you want the model to behave like it's never met you.

276
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
 Useful for testing prompts.

277
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
 Two things to know.

278
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
 Memories are tied to your account only.

279
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
 Nobody else can see them.

280
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
 And anything you don't want stored,

281
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
 you can delete in one click.

282
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
 The trash icon is fast.

283
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,000
 The prompts panel,

284
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
 that's the pencil icon in the left rail,

285
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
 is a personal library of reusable prompts.

286
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
 Things you find yourself typing repeatedly.

287
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
 Click the plus to create one.

288
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,000
 You give it a name,

289
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,000
 optionally a category,

290
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
 the prompt text itself,

291
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,000
 and optionally

292
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
 a slash command that triggers it.

293
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
 With Send Prompts on Select.

294
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
 Checked at the top of the panel,

295
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
 clicking a saved prompt sends it immediately.

296
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
 The rule of thumb.

297
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
 A prompt is a one-shot instruction.

298
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:49,000
 An agent is an ongoing persona.

299
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
 If you find yourself selecting the same prompt every day,

300
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
 that's a sign it should be promoted to an agent instead.

301
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
 Bookmarks are tags you can apply to conversations to find them later.

302
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
 Useful once you have a long chat history.

303
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
 Open any conversation.

304
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
 Click the bookmark icon in the top bar,

305
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
 then New Bookmark.

306
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,000
 Give it a title,

307
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
 typically a category like Project X or Reference,

308
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:15,000
 and optionally a description.

309
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:19,000
 The Add to Current Conversation checkbox tags this chat

310
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
 with the bookmark on the spot.

311
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
 One more thing worth understanding

312
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:27,000
 before you start using MemodoAI for real work.

313
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
 The PII warning.

314
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
 MemodoAI scans your messages for things

315
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
 that look like personal or sensitive data.

316
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
 Credit card numbers.

317
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:38,000
 Social security numbers.

318
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
 Email addresses.

319
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,000
 Phone numbers.

320
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:42,000
 Addresses.

321
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
 Financial identifiers.

322
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
 If the detector finds something,

323
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
 you'll see an orange banner at the top of the screen calling it out.

324
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,000
 I'll demonstrate with a clearly fake test prompt.

325
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,000
 Notice when I send this.

326
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:56,000
 There's the banner.

327
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
 Your message appears to contain personal information.

328
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,000
 It identifies the categories that were flagged.

329
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
 A few important things to know.

330
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
 One, this is a heads up.

331
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
 Not a hard stop.

332
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
 The message still goes to the model.

333
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,000
 So if you notice mid-send that you shouldn't have included something,

334
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
 use the Stop Generating button in the composer to halt the response,

335
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
 then delete the conversation if needed.

336
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
 Two.

337
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
 The detector isn't perfect.

338
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:32,000
 It can flag things that aren't really PII and it can miss things that are.

339
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:36,000
 Treat it as a safety net, not a checkpoint.

340
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:39,000
 You're still responsible for what you send.

341
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,000
 Three.

342
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
 Under the hood.

343
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:46,000
 This is powered by Memodo's PII detection service, redakt.

344
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
 Compliance can adjust its sensitivity.

345
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:50,000
 The simple version.

346
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:54,000
 When the banner appears, stop and read it.

347
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:59,040
 Most of the time, you'll want to edit the message and resend with placeholders

348
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:01,000
 instead of real data.

349
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
 A few quick principles before we wrap up.

350
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
 Be specific.

351
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
 Make this professional is vague.

352
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
 Rewrite this email so it sounds direct but warm.

353
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:16,000
 Three short paragraphs, no greeting, is actionable.

354
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
 The model follows shape better than vibes.

355
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
 Edit before you re-ask.

356
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
 Re-sending a slightly clearer prompt

357
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:28,000
 almost always beats trying to steer the model through a long follow-up.

358
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:29,000
 The model can be wrong.

359
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
 Always check facts that matter.

360
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:36,000
 The model is a very fast drafter, not an oracle.

361
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:37,000
 Sensitive data.

362
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
 Stick to the minimum you need.

363
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:44,000
 If a task doesn't need a real name or a real number, redact first.

364
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:50,000
 The PII warning is a safety net, not a replacement for thinking.

365
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
 That's the tour.

366
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
 Try a few things and don't be shy about building your own agents.

367
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
 That's where the real time savings live.

368
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
 Thanks for watching.

